<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colorado Party (Uruguay)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1836">1836</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Partido Colorado</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> Party is one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>'s two traditional parties, founded in 1836 by supporters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fructuoso-rivera">Fructuoso Rivera</a>. Allied with the liberal and urban interests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>, it fought the Blancos in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguayan-civil-war">Guerra Grande</a> of 1839–1851 and held power almost continuously from 1865 to 1959, notably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-batlle-y-ordonez">José Batlle y Ordóñez</a>. Its most recent president was Jorge Batlle, from 2000 to 2005.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colorado-party-uruguay.jpg" alt="Colorado Party (Uruguay)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vectorised by Froztbyte · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Colorado_Party_(Uruguay).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party's origin is bound up with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fructuoso-rivera">Fructuoso Rivera</a>, a caudillo and leader of the independence process, who was the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> from 1830 to 1834. In 1836 he led a revolt against the second president, Manuel Oribe. The feud had more to do with personal influence than with ideology: Oribe had eliminated a government post from which Rivera exercised patronage over the countryside, and his administration had accused Rivera's cabinet of gross corruption. The battle between their followers in September 1836 produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> (Red) Party and the Blanco (White) Party, named for the colours each side wore. The Blanco tradition later merged with other tendencies to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-uruguay">National Party</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The confrontation was the first of many armed clashes between the two parties through the 19th century. Because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> was a new nation with fragile autonomy, the quarrel became international: the Argentine caudillo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a> and his Federalists supported Oribe, while Rivera's Colorados had the Unitarians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, all enemies of Rosas. The resulting civil war, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguayan-civil-war">Guerra Grande</a>, kept the country divided for almost a decade, with Rivera and his followers confined for most of the 1840s in the walled city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>, besieged by Oribe's Blancos and the armies of Rosas. The Colorado Party was afterwards the dominant party of government almost without exception during the stabilisation of the republic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party has traditionally been ideologically diverse, seeking to unite moderate and liberal groups, with Krausism, liberal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a> and pragmatism among its tendencies. A study of the 1950s noted that the liberal position the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorados</a> took up under Batlle pushed the Blancos towards a relatively more conservative one, though both parties had conservatives and liberals.</p><p>From 1942 to 1994 the Ley de Lemas allowed each party up to three presidential candidates, and a referendum in 1996 limited each to one from the 1999 elections. By then the leftist Broad Front, formed in the early 1970s, had become the most-voted force, and the Colorado Party lost its traditional dominance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Colorado Party (Uruguay)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
